.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
.\" Hand-written (the API frontend parses its arguments by hand, without
.\" clap): what it serves and where it listens.
.TH VELARX-API 8 "" "velarx" "Velarx"
.SH NAME
velarx\-api \- the REST API frontend of the engine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B velarx\-api
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR URI ]...
.RB [ \-\-engine\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-idle\-exit
.IR SECS ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-level
.IR LEVEL ]
.RB [ \-\-start\-engine " | " \-\-no\-start\-engine ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B velarx\-api
serves the REST API for podman and Docker clients (the routes of
.IR /libpod
and of
.IR /api/* )
as a frontend of the engine: it holds no container and no image of its
own, every call goes to the engine service
.RB ( velarx (1)
.IR varlink ),
which it starts when nothing else did
.RB ( \-\-no\-start\-engine
says something else does).
.PP
The sockets come from systemd
.RB ( LISTEN_FDS ),
or are bound here: the unix socket of the configuration
.RI ( [ api ]\ socket
of
.BR velarx.conf (5))
unless
.B \-\-listen
or
.I [api]\ listen
name others.  TCP only when named (see TCP below).  Everything the
process needs from the system is done before it confines itself: the
configuration is read, the sockets are bound, the engine is started if
nothing activates it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-\-config " PATH
The configuration file of the engine
.RI ( default:
.B /etc/velarx/config.toml
as root,
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/velarx/config.toml
otherwise).  The
.I [api]
section is its own.
.TP
.BI "\-\-listen " URI
Where to listen:
.IR unix:///PATH ,
.IR tcp://HOST:PORT ,
.I tcp://:PORT
(the loopback addresses) or the address of the unix socket.  May be given
more than once; the default is
.I [api]\ listen
of the configuration, else its socket.
.TP
.BI "\-\-engine\-socket " PATH
The socket of the engine service, in the place of the configuration's
.RI ( [engine]\ socket ).
.TP
.BI "\-\-idle\-exit " SECS
Leave after this many seconds with no client connected (an engine started
by its socket; the default is
.IR [api]\ idle_exit ,
30).
.TP
.BI "\-\-log\-level " LEVEL
The level of the messages on the standard error
.RI ( default:
.BR warn ).
.TP
.B \-\-start\-engine
Start the engine when none runs (the default when systemd did not start
this process).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-start\-engine
Never start the engine: what starts it is running
.RB ( "velarx varlink \-\-listen\-fd 3" ,
for one).
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and the commit of the build.
.SH TCP
A
.I tcp://HOST:PORT
listener binds the address named (\fItcp://:PORT\fR the loopback addresses).  Whoever can connect to the
port controls the engine with the user of this process (root's engine: the
host), and any local user can connect to a loopback port, so velarx-api does
not start with a TCP listener unless the requests are authenticated: by a
token
.RI ( [api]\ token_file ,
sent as
.BR "Authorization: Bearer TOKEN" ),
by a client certificate
.RB ( \-\-tls\-client\-ca ),
or
.I [api]\ allow_unauthenticated
says that nobody needs one.
.PP
.B \-\-tls\-cert
.IR PEM ,
.B \-\-tls\-key
.I PEM
(or
.IR [api]\ tls_cert ,
.IR tls_key )
serve every TCP listener with TLS 1.3; the certificate file holds the chain.
.B \-\-tls\-client\-ca
.I PEM
(\fItls_client_ca\fR) serves only the clients whose certificate that CA
signed, and the certificate then authenticates: no token is needed.  The
files are read once, at the start.  A listener with no TLS is warned about, and puts the token on the network in clear text on an address
that is not loopback.  The podman client:
.B podman \-\-url tcp://HOST:PORT \-\-tls\-ca CA \-\-tls\-cert CERT \-\-tls\-key KEY
(it speaks https when a TLS option is given).  The unix socket is not
affected.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.I LISTEN_FDS
The sockets systemd passed, from descriptor 3
.RB ( LISTEN_FDNAMES
names them).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A signal asked it to leave, or the last client went and the idle time
passed.
.TP
.B 1
It could not bind a socket, read the configuration or confine itself.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR velarx (1),
.BR velarx.conf (5)
